  "final_message": "I fixed the bug in `session_ttl.py`, and `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` now passes.\n\n**The bug:** line 10 removed the timezone from `now` but not from `created`. With timezone-aware input like `+00:00`, Python then refused to subtract a timestamp without a timezone from one with a timezone (`TypeError: can't subtract offset-naive and offset-aware datetimes`).\n\n**The fix:** both timestamps are now parsed the same way, without removing the timezone:\n```python\nnow = datetime.fromisoformat(now_iso)\n```\n\nI also checked a few cases by hand:\n- 30 seconds elapsed on a 60-second TTL returns `False`.\n- Timestamps in different timezones (`+00:00` and `+02:00`) are compared correctly: 61 seconds elapsed, so `True`.\n- Timestamps without timezones still work as before.\n\nTimestamps with and without a timezone still can't be mixed in one call; that raises the same subtraction error. The README didn't ask for that case, so I left it unchanged. I also didn't touch `format_expiry_message`. The formatter wants to reformat the file; that looks like the existing long line 7, which I left alone to keep the change to one line.\n\nDONE: Stopped removing the timezone from `now` alone, so timezone-aware timestamps compare correctly; the public test passes.",
